module std.prelude
//! Comparison: `Ordering`, the `Ord` and `Eq` traits, the ordering of the primitives,
//! and `min`/`max`/`clamp` over any of them.

// Note on the layout of this file, which holds for the whole prelude: an implementation
// note written as `//` goes *above* the `///` block, never between it and the
// declaration. Attachment is adjacency, so an ordinary comment in between detaches the
// documentation — and says so, via lyra-W017.

// A sum type rather than the -1/0/1 integer Ruby and Perl return: an integer invites
// `if (a <=> b) == -1`, which is strictly worse than `a < b` and would leave the operator
// with no reason to exist.

/// The result of a three-way comparison (`a <=> b`).
///
/// Handling all three outcomes in one place is what `<=>` is for, and exhaustiveness then
/// insists every case is covered — the property the `if`/`else if`/`else` chain it
/// replaces cannot offer.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// match guess <=> secret {
///   Less    => println("Too low!"),
///   Greater => println("Too high!"),
///   Equal   => println("You got it!"),
/// }
/// ```
pub data Ordering =
  /// The left operand sorts before the right.
  Less
  /// The two operands compare equal.
  | Equal
  /// The left operand sorts after the right.
  | Greater

// **Recognized by the `@builtin(Ord)` marker** (08/08), exactly as `Maybe`/`Result` are:
// the marker confers the identity, so the *name* is free and a program's own `trait Ord`
// is an ordinary trait rather than silently becoming the one `<` dispatches through.
// Before the marker the name was the identity, which is the shadowing hazard it removes.
//
// A trait named `Ord` with the right shape is still recognized when *no* marker claims
// the kind anywhere, so a program with no prelude keeps working unchanged.

/// Types that can be ordered.
///
/// Implementing it gives a type `<=>`, and — once the relational operators are routed
/// through it — `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`. `@derive(Ord)` writes the field-wise
/// implementation for you.
///
/// **Floats do not implement it, and that is the rule rather than an omission.** NaN is
/// neither less than, equal to nor greater than anything, so a three-way answer would
/// have to pick one and every choice is wrong — the same reason `<=>` refuses floats
/// outright. Sorting floats wants a `total_cmp` (bit-pattern order) whose name says it is
/// not the arithmetic ordering.
///
/// # Examples
///
/// ```
/// impl Ord for Version {
///   compare = (self, other) => self.major <=> other.major
/// }
/// ```
@builtin(Ord)
pub trait Ord {
  /// How `self` sorts against `other`: `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater`.
  ///
  /// **The only method, deliberately.** Every relational operator derives from it, so an
  /// implementation cannot make `<` and `<=>` disagree — the failure mode C++'s separate
  /// `operator<` and `operator<=>`, and Java's `compareTo` beside `equals`, both carry.
  /// The cost is that a type cannot supply a `<` cheaper than its `compare`; that is a
  /// trade worth making while correctness is the scarce thing.
  compare: (Self, Self) -> Ordering
}

// **`Ord` deliberately does not require `Eq`.** The design first had `trait Ord: Eq`, on
// the reasoning that it stops `compare` answering `Equal` where equality says false. That
// is wrong here: equality is always available structurally, so demanding an *impl* of it
// would make `@derive(Ord)` — which synthesizes no `Eq` — fail on every type that used
// it. A type that implements both and lets them disagree is writing a bug the compiler
// cannot see; that is the residual cost of equality not being a bound.

/// Types whose equality is **not** field-wise.
///
/// **`==` does not require this trait.** Equality is structural by default and works on
/// every type including a bare type variable, so an `Eq` impl *overrides* what `==`
/// already does rather than enabling it — the opposite of Rust and Swift. Implement it
/// for the minority of types that need it: case-insensitive text, a struct with a cache
/// field that should not count, a set that compares by contents rather than by layout.
///
/// A primitive is never routed through an impl — `1 == 1` stays a machine comparison — so
/// an `Eq` impl cannot change equality on the built-in types.
@builtin(Eq)
pub trait Eq {
  /// Whether `self` and `other` are equal, replacing the structural comparison `==` would
  /// otherwise make.
  eq: (Self, Self) -> bool
}

/// Ordering for `string`, so `"a" < "b"` works and a `data` type carrying a string can
/// `@derive(Ord)`.
///
/// **Lexicographic by code point.** A shorter string that is a prefix of a longer one
/// sorts first (`"ab" < "abc"`), which falls out of comparing the common prefix and then
/// the lengths.
///
/// This is **not** a locale-aware or Unicode-collation order: `"Z"` sorts before `"a"`
/// because their code points do, and accented characters sort by code point rather than
/// alphabetically. Real collation needs tables and a locale, and belongs in a Unicode
/// library rather than in the ordering `<` reaches for.
impl Ord for string {
  /// Compares byte by byte, which **is** the code-point order: UTF-8 encodes a lower code
  /// point as a byte sequence that compares lower, so a `memcmp` and a rune-by-rune walk
  /// agree.
  ///
  /// # Complexity
  ///
  /// |            | Best | Average | Worst |
  /// | ---------- | ---- | ------- | ----- |
  /// | **Time**   | O(1) | O(n)    | O(n)  |
  /// | **Memory** | O(1) | O(1)    | O(1)  |
  ///
  /// n is the shorter operand. The best case is a difference in the first byte; the worst
  /// is equal strings, where every byte must be read. That the whole thing is one
  /// `memcmp` is what the byte-order equivalence buys: written in Lyra with `s[i]` the
  /// same comparison would be O(n²), since indexing a string is O(i).
  compare = pure (self, other) => {
    let c = self.compare_bytes(other);
    if c < 0 { Less } else if c > 0 { Greater } else { Equal }
  }
}

// The primitive orderings, and they exist for the same reason `math.lyra`'s arithmetic
// impls do: **so a bound can be satisfied**, not so a call can dispatch. `3 < 5` is a
// machine compare whatever a program declares — a primitive is never routed through an
// impl — so the body's `self <=> other` is the machine operator and not recursion. Without
// them `where t: Ord` is undemandable of a number, which makes `min`, `max`, `clamp` and
// every generic comparison below unwritable for the types that need them most.
//
// **The floats are absent, deliberately.** `<=>` refuses them because NaN is neither less
// than, equal to nor greater than anything, and a three-way answer has to pick one; an
// `Ord` impl for `f64` would be that guess, made once here and inherited by every generic
// that sorts, ranks or picks a maximum. The partial-ordering design (a second `Ordering`
// type, or a fourth variant) is open in `todo.md` and is deferred rather than guessed, so
// `min(1.5, 2.5)` is a compile error today. On concrete floats `if a < b { a } else { b }`
// is a machine compare and works, which is what a program should write meanwhile.
//
// `bool` is absent because `<=>` does not take it, and there is no order on true/false
// worth inventing here.

/// Ordering for `i8`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for i8 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `i16`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for i16 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `i32`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for i32 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `i64`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for i64 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `i128`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for i128 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `u8`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for u8 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `u16`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for u16 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `u32`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for u32 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `u64`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for u64 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `u128`, by the machine comparison `<=>` already makes.
impl Ord for u128 { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }
/// Ordering for `rune`, which is **code-point order** — the order the underlying i32
/// has, and not an alphabetical or locale-aware one, exactly as `impl Ord for string` is
/// lexicographic by code point rather than by collation.
impl Ord for rune { compare = pure noalloc (self, other) => self <=> other }

// **The three below take a `self` receiver**, so `a.min(b)` and `min(a, b)` are the same
// call and an author picks whichever reads better at the site. They are `pure noalloc`,
// so they are usable from code with either bound.
//
// **One sharp edge, on a width narrower or wider than `i64`.** An untyped literal argument
// settles to its default width *before* the type variable is solved (`solveTypeVars`), so
// `count.min(80)` on a `u8` reports *"cannot infer type variable t"* rather than binding
// `t = u8` — the literal has already become an `i64` and the two bindings disagree. The
// spelling that works is `count.min(u8(80))`, and on an `i64` (the default, and what a
// terminal coordinate is) nothing is needed. Solving this wants the untyped argument
// deferred the way an un-annotated lambda already is; it is in `todo.md`.

/// The smaller of two values, or `self` when they compare equal.
///
/// ```
/// let width = requested.min(terminal_width)
/// ```
///
/// **Both spellings work**, because the first parameter is named `self`: `a.min(b)` and
/// `min(a, b)` are the same call. The method form is what reads well in a chain
/// (`count.min(cap).max(0)`); the free form is what reads well when neither argument is
/// the subject.
///
/// **Ties go to `self`, and `max` gives them to `other`** — so `min(a, b)` and `max(a, b)`
/// between them still name *both* values when the two compare equal but are not
/// interchangeable (a struct ordered on one field, say). Had both returned `self`, the
/// pair would answer `a` twice and the other value would simply vanish. It is the same
/// rule Rust's `Ord::min`/`max` follow, for the same reason.
pub let min<t> where t: Ord = pure noalloc (self: t, other: t) -> t =>
  match self.compare(other) { Greater => other, _ => self }

/// The larger of two values, or `other` when they compare equal — see [min] for why the
/// tie goes the other way.
pub let max<t> where t: Ord = pure noalloc (self: t, other: t) -> t =>
  match self.compare(other) { Less => other, Equal => other, Greater => self }

/// `self` restricted to the range `lo..=hi`.
///
/// ```
/// let row = cursor.clamp(0, rows - 1)
/// ```
///
/// **Clamping is the alternative to trapping**, and the reason to reach for it: an index
/// out of bounds is a bug and `xs[i]` traps, but a value being *fitted* to a window is
/// ordinary — a scroll position past the end of a document, a colour past the end of a
/// gradient — and the appropriate answer is the nearest one in range rather than the end
/// of the program.
///
/// # Panics
/// Traps if `lo > hi`, which describes an empty range and so has no nearest value to
/// answer with. Returning either bound would pick one arbitrarily and hide the mistake
/// that produced the reversed pair, which is the silently-wrong answer this language
/// refuses everywhere else. The message is a constant, so `clamp` allocates nothing.
pub let clamp<t> where t: Ord = pure noalloc (self: t, lo: t, hi: t) -> t => {
  match lo.compare(hi) { Greater => panic("clamp: lo is greater than hi"), _ => {} }
  match self.compare(lo) {
    Less => lo,
    _ => match self.compare(hi) { Greater => hi, _ => self },
  }
}
